I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction pattern first, then use the available phase-search and GSAS-II tooling to identify candidates, refine the multiphase model, and verify that all exported results agree numerically.

The sample is a 900 °C Mn–W–O reaction product measured with Cu Kα radiation, so both ternary manganese tungstates and residual/binary oxides are plausible. I’m now checking the search databases and refinement interfaces available in this environment, while extracting the experimental peak positions independently.

The dominant peak set already matches wolframite-type MnWO₄ (including its characteristic low-angle and split 30° region), but several reflections may require a second phase. I’m testing all Mn–W–O, Mn–O, and W–O database structures against the full pattern before deciding whether those peaks are impurities or merely MnWO₄ reflections.

Every statistically significant peak is accounted for by the MnWO₄ reflection list; the alternative Mn₃WO₆ and binary oxide candidates predict strong peaks that are absent. I’m proceeding with a one-phase GSAS-II refinement and will use the refined residual to set an upper limit on any undetected crystalline impurity rather than forcing extra phases into the model.

The initial refinement confirms the single-phase indexing, but the uncalibrated instrument profile parameters are strongly correlated. I’m separating sample broadening from the instrumental terms and checking the residual peak-by-peak so the final model does not claim artificial precision.

A physically sensible sample-displacement correction aligns the peaks and reduces Rwp from about 24% to 12.6%. A weak [110]-type preferred orientation is supported, but it changes Rwp by only ~0.5 percentage point; the remaining mismatch is mainly structure-factor intensity, so I’m now refining the MnWO₄ atomic coordinates while keeping occupancies fixed at stoichiometric values.

The fit now reaches Rwp ≈ 5.26% with physically admissible reflection-geometry corrections and a stable pseudo-Voigt profile. I’m testing the most plausible residual phases (unreacted Mn₃O₄/WO₃, Mn₃WO₆, Mn₂O₃, and MnO) as explicit extra phases; only phases with a positive, statistically supported scale will be retained.

The trace-phase tests are internally consistent across reasonable background choices: the best supported model is ~98.3 wt% MnWO₄ with sub‑1 wt% Mn₂O₃, Mn₃O₄, and WO₃. Their scales improve χ² significantly, but their fractions vary with profile choice, so I’ll retain them as tentative trace phases, keep their literature cells/coordinates fixed, and report the formal Rietveld uncertainties separately from that larger systematic uncertainty.

The final refinement converged at Rwp = 5.225% and the crystalline-normalized fractions sum to 1.000000. I’m doing a final consistency audit now: reloading the submitted GPX, reparsing every exported CIF, and numerically comparing fit.csv and result.json against the arrays and covariance stored in final.gpx.

